From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 16:27:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(render): reproduce native classic cloud distance fade

Java 26.3 keeps clouds visible across a separate 2048-block cloud range,
which leaves distant clouds in Hive and CubeCraft views where the official
Bedrock client has already faded them out.

Reproduce Bedrock 1.26.51.1 Clouds.material vertex alpha:
clamp(1 - max(distance / D - 0.9, 0), 0, 1). The official executable
(SHA-256 537c0aee2e79afbdc94b44b28e00f466ae62bc50e2733d953b430db9dbaa9ee7)
and material (99cc015e2668a381f759a1aa3571b30926c52ac0724451a2938dbfdf62363818)
establish per-vertex spherical distance and unchanged fragment RGBA.
A hardware observation of native gameplay cloud draw 144714d90 confirms
configured ten chunks produce camera range 176, margin distance 160,
and final cloud distance 160. Do not subtract the native extra chunk
again from Java's effective terrain range.

Use dedicated Fancy pipelines, including all OIT stages, with existing
geometry, colors, bindings and render state. Preserve the depth-bounds
stage and avoid applying Java's fragment fade a second time. Activate
through accepted Bedrock rendering context only, for dry ordinary
Overworld air with Fancy clouds and at least five effective chunks.
Keep weather, effects, boss fog, Fast clouds and unverified low-distance
branches on the existing path. Cloud height and dimension semantics do
not change without versioned evidence.

Five targeted regressions verify scope and render-state preservation.
The actual authored vertex shader matches an independent native fade
reference for 20160 GPU vertices, including OIT depth bounds.
